    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by using the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). Using this formula, we can set up the following equations:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3\\) (since \\(27 = 3^3\\)), we use the logarithm power rule \\(\\log_b (b^k) = k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = \\log_3 3 = 1\n\\]\n\nThis transforms our original equations to:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nWe know from earlier that \\(\\log 4 = \\log (2^2) = 2 \\log 2\\) and that \\(\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3 \\log 3\\). Plugging these into the above equations, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3}\n\\]\n\nThe second equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction unless \\(\\log x = 3\\). Thus, we need to determine \\(\\log x\\). We find that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log x}{2 \\log 2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n\\log x = 6 \\log 2\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(x = 2^6 = 64\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
